WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.080
Peter Parker can't save everyone —&nbsp;
but in "Spider-Man: No Way Home,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:07.200
one Peter Parker gets himself a second chance.

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:12.000
Peter Parker, aka Spider-Man, is one Marvel&nbsp;
character who is as defined by his love&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:17.600
interests as his powers; he has had some of the&nbsp;
most iconic girlfriends in all of Marvel Comics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:21.840
From MJ Watson to Gwen Stacy,&nbsp;
Peter sure is one lucky fella.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:24.880
And that's not even getting into&nbsp;
his various flings with Black Cat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:29.920
aka Felicia Hardy. Any time the web-slinger&nbsp;
receives a new big-screen adaptation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:34.800
a big question on everyone's mind is which of&nbsp;
his girlfriends will be present in the film.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:38.400
"You're wrong about us being on different paths.&nbsp;
We're not on different paths. You're my path.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:42.240
And you're always gonna be my path."

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:47.360
The newest version of the character, played by Tom&nbsp;
Holland, got a reboot to his romantic life with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:53.200
2017's "Spider-Man: Homecoming." Initially,&nbsp;
Peter Parker has a crush on classmate Liz,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:56.240
but things get pretty complicated&nbsp;
when it turns out that her dad is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.240 --> 00:01:00.400
a villain known as "Vulture." By the time&nbsp;
"Spider-Man: Far From Home" rolls around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:04.960
we watch as Peter finally starts&nbsp;
going out with Michelle Jones, aka MJ.

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:09.440
Their relationship reaches a new level in&nbsp;
"Spider-Man: No Way Home" when they officially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:13.520
become a couple. But being Spider-Man's&nbsp;
girlfriend comes with more than the perk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:18.960
of dating a superhero. As MJ quickly learns,&nbsp;
it often means getting put in harm's way, too.

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:22.640
During the climactic "No Way Home"&nbsp;
battle against Electro, Sandman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:27.920
and Lizard, MJ finds herself in a position many of&nbsp;
Spider-Man's girlfriends have encountered before.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:32.080
Left to fend for herself on a tall&nbsp;
structure as her boyfriend battles villains,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:36.720
MJ falls off and plummets toward what&nbsp;
appears to be certain death. But then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:40.640
something interesting happens that's&nbsp;
bound to give fans a sense of deja vu.

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:44.320
Arguably, one of the most definitive&nbsp;
moments of Andrew Garfield's tenure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:49.280
as Spider-Man is when he fails to catch&nbsp;
Gwen Stacy as she falls down a clock tower&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:54.080
while he fights Harry Osborn in "The Amazing&nbsp;
Spider-Man 2." The death of Gwen Stacy is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:58.880
major event in Marvel Comics that is translated&nbsp;
to become an equally major onscreen event that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:03.200
likely caused more than one audience&nbsp;
member to shed a tear back in 2014.

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:07.520
The event caused Garfield's Spider-Man&nbsp;
to temporarily hang up the suit and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:11.040
stop being the friendly neighborhood&nbsp;
hero the city needed. He comes back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:14.160
around to fighting Rhino by the end&nbsp;
of the film, but it's clear from his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:18.240
most recent appearance that he's still&nbsp;
not quite over Gwen's death just yet.

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:22.560
When Tom Holland's Peter Parker is at his&nbsp;
lowest, the Garfield variant lets him know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:27.680
about how he lost his girlfriend some time ago.&nbsp;
He doesn't go into detail, but we, the audience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:32.000
know who he's referring to. We know this&nbsp;
is a Spider-Man haunted by his mistakes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:35.360
and when a similar moment transpires&nbsp;
in "No Way Home," he doesn't throw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:38.800
away the opportunity to prevent&nbsp;
history from repeating itself.

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:42.720
When MJ falls off the Statue of Liberty,&nbsp;
Holland's Spider-Man jumps to save her but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:48.000
is stopped short by Green Goblin. Fortunately,&nbsp;
Garfield's Spider-Man sees what's happening and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:53.360
leaps into action. He catches MJ, allowing her&nbsp;
to land safely on the ground. The scene lingers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:57.440
on the superhero for a beat, letting the audience&nbsp;
really take in what's happened. He may not have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:02.400
been able to save Gwen, but he prevented another&nbsp;
Spider-Man from losing the person he loves most.

